Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama; naturalistic faces, fog, candlelight, wool and frost; one consistent live-action look for all cast and spirits.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Dense brown fog. Snow and soot. A black warehouse door bears the painted legend: SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

(SOUND: distant church bell, wheels on wet stone, muffled coughs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A mean office iced with cold. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, thin, pointed nose, blue thin lips, frosty rime on brows and wiry chin, black coat, sits at a high desk over ledgers. Beyond, in a dismal little cell, BOB CRATCHIT, threadbare, white comforter, copies letters at a fire of one coal.

(SOUND: pen scratch, fog-muffled street noise, coal settling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grind-stone, Scrooge! a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous, old sinner!

The street door opens. FRED, young, ruddy, handsome, glowing from the frost, strides in.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew, keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then.

FRED
I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes out his last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle! And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves warmly, greeting Bob at the door. Two portly charity gentlemen enter with books and papers, hats off. MR. GRANTHAM leads.

MR. GRANTHAM
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

MR. GRANTHAM
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

MR. GRANTHAM
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Are there no prisons?

MR. GRANTHAM
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

MR. GRANTHAM
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Fog thickens at the panes.

(SOUND: church tower bell striking through fog)

At shutting time Scrooge buttons his great-coat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob snuffs his candle, winds his long white comforter, and hurries out.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS YARD - NIGHT

A lowering pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang on the black gateway. Scrooge gropes to his door, key in hand. The large knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE: motionless wide eyes, spectacles on ghostly forehead, livid, hair stirred as by hot air.

(SOUND: wind in the yard, a low unearthly hum)

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key sturdily, enters, lights a candle.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - HALL AND STAIRS - NIGHT

Wide dark stairs. Scrooge trims his dip and climbs, glancing back.

(SOUND: door bang like thunder, echoes through empty rooms)

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom beyond. Small fire, saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, in dressing-gown, slippers, nightcap, double-locks the door and sits to his gruel. Dutch tiles around the grate. A disused bell begins to swing, then rings; every bell in the house answers.

(SOUND: soft bell, then peal of all house bells, then dead silence)

(SOUND: clanking chain dragged over cellar casks, rising up the stairs)

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy door is no barrier. MARLEY'S GHOST passes through: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots; transparent; chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds clasped about his middle. The dying flame leaps up.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite the fire. Hair and tassels stir as by oven vapour.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It removes the bandage; the lower jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

(SOUND: appalling chain rattle, jaw clack)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles, glancing at the floor.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—mark me!—in life. Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business!

It flings the chain upon the ground.

(SOUND: heavy chain strike on floorboards)

MARLEY'S GHOST
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY'S GHOST
You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds its jaw, walks backward; the window rises. Outside, phantoms in chains wail in the air. Marley joins the dirge and floats out. Scrooge shuts the window, tries the double-locked door, and staggers to bed.

(SOUND: mournful wailing on the wind, then silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Darkness. Church chimes. The heavy bell tolls ONE. Light flashes. Bed-curtains drawn aside by a hand.

(SOUND: deep hollow ONE; sudden rush of light)

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST: childlike yet old, white hair, unwrinkled face, bare arms and feet, white tunic, lustrous belt, holly branch, summer flowers; jet of light from its head; extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure flickers—now one arm, now many legs—then whole again.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. They move toward the window.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit's hand on his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD AND SCHOOL - DAY

Clear cold winter day, snow on the ground. Open road, fields, a little market-town in the distance. Boys on ponies shout Merry Christmas. A dull red-brick schoolhouse, broken fortunes, mossy walls.

(SOUND: crisp snow underfoot, boys' distant shouts, winter birds)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us. The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. SCHOOLROOM - DAY

Long bare room, deal forms and desks. BOY SCROOGE, lonely, reads near a feeble fire. Adult Scrooge sits on a form and weeps.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from a water-spout, idle door swing)

A little girl, FAN, much younger, darts in and embraces the boy.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart! She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

A snug bright warehouse cleared for a ball. OLD FEZZIWIG, Welsh wig, capacious waistcoat, jovial, claps from a high desk. Young apprentice SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS haul shutters. A fiddler tunes. MRS. FEZZIWIG and daughters, employees, housemaid, baker, milkman pour in. Dancing, cake, cold roast, Sir Roger de Coverley. Fezziwig cuts a deft step.

(SOUND: fiddle reel, laughter, hand-claps, boots on boards)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads!

At eleven, Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig shake every hand at the door with Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. CITY STREET / INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - EVENING

Younger man SCROOGE, prime of life, eager greedy eye, sits with BELLE in mourning-dress; tears in her eyes in the Spirit's light.

(SOUND: muffled street traffic, soft fire)

BELLE
It matters little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. Another shadow: Belle now a comely matron by a winter fire, children tumultuous and happy; her husband arrives with Christmas toys. He mentions seeing Scrooge alone in his office, partner dying.

HUSBAND
Mr. Scrooge it was. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! I don't wish to see it. Remove me! I cannot bear it!

Scrooge seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down on the Spirit's head. Light still streams from under it. He reels, exhausted, into his own bedroom and heavy sleep.

(SOUND: struggle, then silence and bedclothes)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM AND SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

The bell upon the stroke of One. Ruddy light from the adjoining room. Scrooge shuffles to the door. His sitting-room is transformed: living green, holly, mistletoe, ivy; mighty blaze; throne of turkeys, geese, pigs, puddings, oysters, fruit, seething punch. Upon it sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT: jolly Giant in green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with icicles, glowing torch like Plenty's horn.

(SOUND: roaring fire, punch simmer, rich room hush)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family; meaning my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I don't think I have. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Snow on roofs, cheerful scrapers, poulterers and grocers radiant, church bells, poor people carrying dinners to bakers. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch; angry jostlers soften.

(SOUND: bells, shovels on snow, market bustle, laughter)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most. Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, poorly dressed but brave in ribbons, lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER in monstrous borrowed collar. Two smaller Cratchits dance for the goose. MARTHA arrives; they hide her as BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM upon his shoulder—little crutch, iron frame.

(SOUND: kettle, children's shrieks of joy, crutch on floorboards)

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim!

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs from the closet into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tim off to hear the pudding.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Goose, apple-sauce, mashed potatoes; then the pudding like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing with brandy and holly. After, chestnuts and hot mixture in two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle.

(SOUND: hiss of gravy, crackle of chestnuts, family chatter)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim close at Bob's side. Bob holds his withered hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. It should be Christmas Day on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him!

The shadow lifts; they grow merry again. The Spirit's torch brightens them as they fade.

INT. FRED'S DRAWING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright dry gleaming room. FRED laughs until he holds his sides. His pretty NIECE and friends roar with him. Topper eyes the plump sister.

(SOUND: hearty laughter, fire, later harp and glee)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him. I mean to give him the same chance every year. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it if he finds me going there, year after year. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Uncle Scrooge! A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man!

Scrooge, invisible, softens and would have thanked them.

EXT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

The Ghost has grown old; hair grey. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven. From its robe cling two children: yellow, meagre, ragged, wolfish—a boy and a girl.

(SOUND: distant chimes, cold wind)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone. A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, comes like mist along the ground—the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME. Only one outstretched hand is visible.

(SOUND: twelve deep strokes; then dead hush)

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The garment contracts as if the Spirit nods. Scrooge's legs tremble.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company. Lead on! Lead on, Spirit!

EXT. THE EXCHANGE - DAY

Merchants chink money, look at watches. The Spirit points to a knot of men.

(SOUND: coins, boots, indifferent chatter)

FAT MERCHANT
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. BENTLEY
When did he die?

FAT MERCHANT
Last night, I believe.

MR. CROSS
What was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

FAT MERCHANT
God knows. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. Upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

They laugh and stroll away. Elsewhere two wealthy men:

MR. WORTLEY
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. PEEL
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

Low beetling shop: iron, rags, bones. OLD JOE by a charcoal stove. CHARWOMAN, MRS. DILBER the laundress, and UNDERTAKER'S MAN enter with bundles and burst out laughing.

(SOUND: rusty hinge shriek, pipe draw, coarse laughter)

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

CHARWOMAN
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did. Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose. Open that bundle, old Joe.

Plunder: seals, pencil-case, sheets, teaspoons, then bed-curtains, blankets, and the dead man's best shirt.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not? I certainly shan't hold my hand for the sake of such a man as He was. Somebody was fool enough to put it on him to be buried in, but I took it off again.

CHARWOMAN
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body, plundered, unwatched, unwept. Pale light on the cover. A cat at the door; rats beneath the hearth.

(SOUND: cat scratch, gnawing rats, terrible stillness)

The Spirit points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' SITTING-ROOM - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, anxious, with children. Her careworn husband enters.

(SOUND: clock tick, children's hushed play)

CAROLINE
Is it good, or bad?

HUSBAND
Bad.

CAROLINE
We are quite ruined?

HUSBAND
No. There is hope yet, Caroline. He is past relenting. He is dead. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Their hearts are lighter. The only emotion shown is pleasure at the death.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - EVENING

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and daughters sew. Peter reads. The noisy Cratchits are still as statues.

(SOUND: soft fire, a single suppressed sob)

PETER
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world.

Bob enters, comforter on, cheerful yet broken. He praises their work for the funeral. He goes upstairs, sits by the child's place, kisses the little face, returns.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim—shall we—or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Walled churchyard, grass and weeds, choked with burying. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one neglected stone.

(SOUND: wind in yews, distant iron gate)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Spirit points downward still.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

Scrooge creeps forward and reads: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger points from grave to him and back.

SCROOGE
Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the hand. The Phantom repulses him. The hood and dress shrink, collapse, and dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. Soft daylight. Scrooge tangled in bed-curtains, sobbing then laughing, face wet.

(SOUND: church bells pealing lustily—clash, clang, ding-dong)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He frisks into the sitting-room.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down—rings and all. I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He runs to the window, opens it. Clear bright cold; golden sunlight; merry bells. A boy in Sunday clothes below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there—not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's hanging there now? Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge dressed in his best, smiling at all. He meets the portly MR. GRANTHAM.

(SOUND: crowd, bright footsteps, greetings)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

MR. GRANTHAM
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it. Will you come and see me?

MR. GRANTHAM
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - AFTERNOON

Table in great array. Scrooge sidles his face round the door.

(SOUND: warm room murmur, then burst of welcome)

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?—Let him in!

Hearty shaking of hands. Wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine; quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half late, sheds hat and comforter and drives his pen.

(SOUND: clock strike, frantic pen, stool scrape)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool and digs Bob in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob staggers, reaches toward the ruler, then understands.

EXT. LONDON - MONTAGE - DAY

Scrooge with Tiny Tim walking, Tim not dying; Scrooge a second father. Laughing in the streets; some laugh at him, he lets them. Church and home and open hand.

(SOUND: bright street life, Tim's crutch and laugh, fading into soft bells)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew. His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed—

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
